Patch 7929 (rollback netcode, online stalls): the game window and its message pump run on their own thread; HITCH also times the pipe wait and Present.

Why. The window belonged to the port's frame clock thread, the thread that drains the FIFO and gives the guest its vblanks. Its pump
called DispatchMessage, and DefWindowProc runs its modal loops in place: holding the title bar or a frame edge (move / size), the window
menu (Alt, Alt+Space, a click on the window icon), and any slow message another process sends to the window (an overlay, a global hook).
For as long as such a loop runs the frame clock gives no vblank and takes no flip, so the guest stops; in a rollback match the lockstep
clock then stops both PCs. One online match (test10 friends' bundle b, room 69) had a 897 ms tick whose frame clock time was 865 ms of
window pump.

What. create_window now runs on a new thread (win_thread_main) that does nothing else than GetMessage / DispatchMessage for that window.
The modal loops wait there, not on the frame clock. The frame clock's pump (rsx_d3d12_backend_pump_messages) still empties its own
queue and reports the closed flag, which the window thread also sets when its loop ends. The thread runs one step above normal unless
HOST_PRIO=0: the submit thread's SetWindowText (the title bar stats) and Present can send it a message and wait for the answer. It
never waits on a drawing thread, so a sent message cannot deadlock.

Closing (review of test11). Esc, the close button and rsx_d3d12_request_close set the closed flag and hide the window; they do not
destroy it, because the submit thread may be inside Present or SetWindowTextA for it at that moment. Shutdown destroys it on its own
thread (a private WM_APP message; DestroyWindow only works on the owner thread). If the backend failed to initialise after the window
was made (no device or swap chain), main.cpp never polls the closed flag, so a close then ends the process, as "End task" on the
unanswered window did before.

Unchanged: the guest sees nothing new (presentation and input only). Keys are read with GetAsyncKeyState and the focus is checked by
process (GetForegroundWindow + process id), both independent of the thread that owns the window. The in-game text panel's calls from
the window procedure take its own lock (cellOskDialog.c s_ov_lock). The swap chain is created for the window from the frame clock
thread, as DXGI allows. WINDOW_THREAD=0 keeps the old way (the window on the frame clock thread). One line at start says which.

Diagnostic. With HITCH=<ms> (2905), the per-present line also shows "pipe wait" (the frame clock waiting for the submit thread to finish
the previous batch, which includes its Present and its next-back-buffer wait) and "Present" (the Present call itself, on the submit
thread). Off by default, as before.

